Cortex-M0+ single-stepping no longer works after the refactor

Our `rp2040js` emulator could run small Thumb programs and handle their memory accesses and interrupts before the refactor. I use that path to exercise Pico firmware without hardware, but the core execution APIs now stop at placeholders.

For example, this comparison should be possible through the normal stepping API:

```ts
import { RP2040 } from 'rp2040js';
const pico = new RP2040();
pico.core.PC = 0x20000100;
pico.core.registers[0] = 9;
pico.writeUint16(pico.core.PC, 0x2807); // CMP r0, #7
pico.step();
```

This should execute the comparison and update the core's status flags and program counter. Currently, the execution path throws `Error("not implemented")` instead.

Could you restore instruction execution through `RP2040.step` and `CortexM0Core.executeInstruction`, including the supporting register access, boot-vector initialization, memory writes, and interrupt entry and return? The throwing stubs are in `src/cortex-m0-core.ts`, `src/rp2040.ts`, `src/peripherals/ppb.ts`, and `src/peripherals/peripheral.ts`. Also restore the missing exports `opcodeBX`, `opcodeMOVS`, `opcodeNOP`, `opcodePOP`, and `opcodePUSH` in `src/utils/assembler.ts` so callers can construct programs again; their parameters and Thumb encoding behaviour are documented in `FEATURE.md`.

